content.text:
Cs3DyO3 (mp-779546) is the only phase I'm reporting. I placed the simulated peaks by counting along the grid by hand, so positions could be off by a step or so.

- **Matches:** the Cs3DyO3 simulation lines up with the observed peaks, with the shift growing slightly at higher angle, which fits a slightly compressed lattice:
  - a broad feature near 25.3° (observed about 25.45°);
  - 26.65° (observed 26.7°);
  - the strongest peak at 27.65° (observed 27.7°);
  - 29.2° and 29.6° (observed 29.3° and 29.75°);
  - about 31.05° (observed 31.15°);
  - about 32.65° (observed 32.85°).
- **Weak spot:** the low-angle peak near 16° fits less well. The simulation puts it at about 16.07°, the observation at 16.25°, and it is stronger in the observed pattern (39 against about 17).
- **Other candidates:** none of the other eight puts its strong lines where the observed peaks are. KAuO2 and Li2MnFeO4 have strong low-angle lines that aren't observed, and LuTaO4, BaPr2CuO5, AgPdO2, VAg3O4, BaNaGdWO6 and Sm2ZnPtO6 have their main peaks in the wrong places. Nothing I saw needed a second phase.

{"present": ["mp-779546"], "confidence": 0.72, "note": "Observed peaks near 25.4, 26.7, 27.7 (strongest), 29.3, 29.75, 31.15 and 32.85 degrees match the simulated Cs3DyO3 pattern with a small positive shift, and no other candidate fits."}

